description:
There is already a full-featured fields directory screen at `mobile_app/app/fields/index.tsx` (list + search + infinite scroll, pushes to `/field/[id]`) and it's currently reachable only via a chip inside the search tab (`mobile_app/app/(tabs)/search.tsx:355-366`, `onPress={() => router.push('/fields')}`). The user wants a direct entry point in the bottom tab bar too. Depends on task 1 (`t('tabs.fields')`).

Mirror the exact pattern this codebase already uses for the center "create" tab (a placeholder route file + a custom `tabBarButton` that intercepts the press and pushes a stack route directly, so the tab never actually renders its own placeholder content):

1. Create `mobile_app/app/(tabs)/fields.tsx` (new file), copying the shape of `mobile_app/app/(tabs)/create.tsx` exactly:
```tsx
import { Redirect } from 'expo-router';

/** Tab slot for the Fields directory — navigation is handled in the tab bar button. */
export default function FieldsTabPlaceholder() {
  return <Redirect href="/fields" />;
}
```

2. In `mobile_app/app/(tabs)/_layout.tsx`, add a new `<Tabs.Screen name="fields" .../>` as the LAST screen in the `<Tabs>` block, i.e. immediately after the existing `<Tabs.Screen name="friends" .../>` block (currently ends at line 179) and before the closing `</Tabs>` (line 180). Use the same `TabBarIcon` helper already defined in this file (line 15-20) with FontAwesome icon `"th-list"` (the same icon already used for the "מגרשים" chip in `search.tsx:361`, so the affordance reads consistently across the app), and a custom `tabBarButton` that pushes `/fields` directly instead of letting the navigator mount `FieldsTabPlaceholder` (mirrors the `create` screen's `tabBarButton` at lines 147-158, minus the FAB-specific circular styling — this one should look like a normal tab, not a floating button):
```tsx
      <Tabs.Screen
        name="fields"
        options={{
          title: t('tabs.fields'),
          tabBarIcon: ({ color }) => <TabBarIcon name="th-list" color={color} />,
          tabBarButton: (props) => (
            <TouchableOpacity
              accessibilityRole="button"
              accessibilityLabel={t('tabs.fields')}
              activeOpacity={0.7}
              onPress={() => router.push('/fields')}
              style={props.style}
              className="items-center justify-center"
            >
              {props.children}
            </TouchableOpacity>
          ),
        }}
      />
```
`TouchableOpacity`, `router`, and `t` are already imported/available in this file (see the existing `create` screen block and the top-level `const router = useRouter();` / `const { t } = useTranslation();`) — no new imports needed.

Resulting tab order (array order; note this is an RTL app so the FIRST array item renders on the visual RIGHT): games (index), search, [create FAB], chats, friends, fields — so "fields" lands at the visual far-left edge (a side position), while "games" keeps its existing prominent first/rightmost slot and the center "create" FAB (itself game-creation) continues to anchor the middle of the bar. Do not reorder the other existing `Tabs.Screen` entries.

The web fields list (`next_app/src/components/FieldsBrowser.tsx:18-23,86-125`) has a horizontal row of sport-filter chips (הכל / כדורגל / כדורסל / טניס) above the results, wired into the fetch's `sport` query param. The mobile directory screen (`mobile_app/app/fields/index.tsx`) currently has only the search box (lines 99-109) with no sport filter, even though `fieldsApi.getPage` already accepts a `sport` param (`mobile_app/src/services/api/fields.ts:129-144`, forwarded to the server as `?sport=`) and the shared sport metadata (`SPORT_MAPPING`, `SPORT_EMOJI`) is already available via `@/utils/sports` (re-exported from `@joinup/shared/sports`, same source `next_app`'s `FieldsBrowser.tsx` uses).

1. Add near the top of the file (module scope, alongside `PAGE_SIZE`):
```ts
import { SPORT_MAPPING, SPORT_EMOJI } from '@/utils/sports';

type SportFilter = 'ALL' | 'SOCCER' | 'BASKETBALL' | 'TENNIS';
const FILTERS: { label: string; value: SportFilter }[] = [
    { label: 'הכל', value: 'ALL' },
    { label: SPORT_MAPPING.SOCCER, value: 'SOCCER' },
    { label: SPORT_MAPPING.BASKETBALL, value: 'BASKETBALL' },
    { label: SPORT_MAPPING.TENNIS, value: 'TENNIS' },
];
```
2. Add `const [sportFilter, setSportFilter] = useState<SportFilter>('ALL');` next to the existing `query`/`debouncedQuery` state (line 19-20).
3. In `fetchPage` (lines 31-48), pass the filter through: `fieldsApi.getPage({ take: PAGE_SIZE, skip, q: debouncedQuery, sport: sportFilter })`, and add `sportFilter` to the `useCallback` dependency array on line 48 (`[debouncedQuery, sportFilter]`). Since the existing reset effect (lines 50-52, `useEffect(() => { fetchPage(0, false); }, [fetchPage]);`) already re-runs whenever `fetchPage` is recreated, this alone makes a filter change trigger a fresh fetch-from-scratch — no other effect changes needed.
4. Render the filter chips row directly below the existing search box block (after line 109's closing `</View>`, before the `{loading ? ... }` block at line 111): a horizontal `ScrollView` of `TouchableOpacity` pills, matching this file's existing visual language (rounded-full, brand color when selected) rather than importing MUI concepts from web:
```tsx
<View className="px-4 pb-3 border-b border-gray-100">
    <ScrollView horizontal showsHorizontalScrollIndicator={false}>
        {FILTERS.map((f) => {
            const selected = sportFilter === f.value;
            const emoji = f.value !== 'ALL' ? SPORT_EMOJI[f.value] : undefined;
            return (
                <TouchableOpacity
                    key={f.value}
                    onPress={() => setSportFilter(f.value)}
                    className={`mr-2 px-4 py-1.5 rounded-full border ${selected ? 'bg-brand border-brand' : 'bg-white border-gray-300'}`}
                >
                    <Text className={selected ? 'text-white font-bold text-sm' : 'text-gray-600 text-sm'}>
                        {emoji ? `${emoji} ${f.label}` : f.label}
                    </Text>
                </TouchableOpacity>
            );
        })}
    </ScrollView>
</View>
```
Add `ScrollView` to the existing `react-native` import on line 1 (it currently imports `View, Text, TouchableOpacity, TextInput, Image, FlatList, ActivityIndicator` — add `ScrollView` to that list).
5. Update the empty-state message (lines 115-121) to distinguish "no results for this filter/search" from "no fields at all", mirroring web's `FieldsBrowser.tsx:143-147`: when `debouncedQuery || sportFilter !== 'ALL'`, keep using `t('field.emptyDirectory')`; there is no separate translation key for the filtered-vs-unfiltered distinction today, so it's fine to reuse the same `t('field.emptyDirectory')` string for both cases — do not invent a new translation key for this, it's a minor nicety, not required for parity.

The backend favorites API already exists and is unused by mobile: `server/routes/users.js:230-264` exposes `GET /api/users/:id/favorites` (returns `Field[]`, confirmed at line 233: `res.json(rows.map(r => r.field))`), `POST /api/users/:id/favorites/:fieldId` (auth required, upserts, returns the join row), and `DELETE /api/users/:id/favorites/:fieldId` (auth required, returns `{ ok: true }`). No server changes needed — this task only adds the mobile client wrapper, mirroring the style of every other method already in this file (e.g. `getFriends`, `removeFriend` at lines 141-143, 182-187).

1. Add `import type { Field } from './fields';` near the top of the file (alongside the existing `import type { FieldFlagReason } from './fields';` on line 2 — combine into one import or add a second `import type` line, either is fine as long as both types are imported).
2. Add three new methods to the `usersApi` object (e.g. near `getFriends`/`removeFriend`, since favorites are a similar per-user relation):
```ts
    getFavorites: (userId: string) => {
        return apiClient<Field[]>(`/api/users/${userId}/favorites`, { cache: 'no-store' });
    },

    addFavorite: (userId: string, fieldId: string, token: string) => {
        return apiClient(`/api/users/${userId}/favorites/${fieldId}`, { method: 'POST', token });
    },

    removeFavorite: (userId: string, fieldId: string, token: string) => {
        return apiClient(`/api/users/${userId}/favorites/${fieldId}`, { method: 'DELETE', token });
    },
```
`getFavorites` intentionally takes no token — the server route has no `authenticateToken` middleware (line 230), matching `apiClient`'s existing optional-token calling convention used elsewhere in this file (e.g. compare to `getProfile` which does pass a token because its route requires one — check `apiClient`'s signature in `./client.ts` if unsure, but do not add an auth requirement the server route doesn't have).

Port `next_app/src/components/FavoriteButton.tsx` to React Native, for use in task 6 on both the fields list and field detail screens. Reuse the exact same module-level-cached-promise pattern the web version uses (lines 10-32 of the web file) so a screen with many field cards only fetches the user's favorites list once, not once per card.

Create `mobile_app/src/components/FavoriteButton.tsx`:
```tsx
import React, { useEffect, useState } from 'react';
import { TouchableOpacity, ActivityIndicator } from 'react-native';
import FontAwesome from '@expo/vector-icons/FontAwesome';
import { useAuth } from '@clerk/clerk-expo';
import { usersApi } from '@/services/api';
import type { Field } from '@/services/api/fields';

let globalFavoritesPromise: Promise<Field[]> | null = null;
let globalFavoritesUserId: string | null = null;

function getFavorites(userId: string): Promise<Field[]> {
    if (globalFavoritesPromise && globalFavoritesUserId === userId) {
        return globalFavoritesPromise;
    }
    globalFavoritesUserId = userId;
    globalFavoritesPromise = usersApi.getFavorites(userId).catch(() => {
        globalFavoritesPromise = null;
        return [];
    });
    return globalFavoritesPromise;
}

function invalidateFavorites() {
    globalFavoritesPromise = null;
}

export default function FavoriteButton({ fieldId, size = 20 }: { fieldId: string; size?: number }) {
    const { userId, getToken } = useAuth();
    const [isFav, setIsFav] = useState(false);
    const [loading, setLoading] = useState(false);

    useEffect(() => {
        if (!userId) return;
        let cancelled = false;
        getFavorites(userId).then((arr) => {
            if (!cancelled) setIsFav(arr.some((f) => f.id === fieldId));
        });
        return () => { cancelled = true; };
    }, [userId, fieldId]);

    if (!userId) return null;

    const toggle = async () => {
        if (loading) return;
        setLoading(true);
        try {
            const token = await getToken();
            if (!token) return;
            if (!isFav) {
                await usersApi.addFavorite(userId, fieldId, token);
                setIsFav(true);
            } else {
                await usersApi.removeFavorite(userId, fieldId, token);
                setIsFav(false);
            }
            invalidateFavorites();
        } catch (e) {
            console.error('Failed to toggle favorite', e);
        } finally {
            setLoading(false);
        }
    };

    return (
        <TouchableOpacity
            onPress={toggle}
            disabled={loading}
            accessibilityRole="button"
            accessibilityLabel={isFav ? 'הסר ממועדפים' : 'הוסף למועדפים'}
            hitSlop={{ top: 8, bottom: 8, left: 8, right: 8 }}
            style={{
                backgroundColor: 'rgba(255,255,255,0.9)',
                borderRadius: 999,
                width: size + 14,
                height: size + 14,
                alignItems: 'center',
                justifyContent: 'center',
            }}
        >
            {loading ? (
                <ActivityIndicator size="small" color="#f59e0b" />
            ) : (
                <FontAwesome name={isFav ? 'star' : 'star-o'} size={size} color={isFav ? '#f59e0b' : '#475569'} />
            )}
        </TouchableOpacity>
    );
}
```
Verify `usersApi` is exported from `mobile_app/src/services/api/index.ts` (it almost certainly already is, alongside `fieldsApi` — check the file, do not assume) and that `@clerk/clerk-expo`'s `useAuth()` exposes `userId` directly (it does elsewhere in this codebase, e.g. `mobile_app/app/field/[id].tsx:47`, though there it's used together with `getToken` — same hook, just also destructure `userId` here). This component depends on task 4's `usersApi.getFavorites/addFavorite/removeFavorite`.
